    "narration": "[NARRATOR] (Warm, distinctly Californian, values-driven)\nBefore Viral Fusion becomes technology—\nlegislation—\nor infrastructure—\nit eventually comes down to something much more personal.\nHow people live.\nHow they feed their families.\nWhether they can afford to stay in their communities.\nWhether businesses can continue investing there.\nAnd whether citizens believe they still have a meaningful voice in the decisions shaping those things.\n\n[NARRATOR] (Warm, distinctly Californian, values-driven)\nThat brings us to one of the most recognizable family-owned brands California has ever produced.\nIn-N-Out Burger.\nAnd its owner and president—\nLynsi Snyder.\n\n[GEORGE]\nI would start this conversation with the food.\nBeef.\nFarmers.\nRanchers.\nFood prices.\nEnvironmental regulation.\nAlternative proteins.\nCultivated meat.\nAnd consumer choice.\nNot with a predetermined answer.\nWith a question:\nWho should decide the future of food—the government, the producer, the market, or the consumer?\nAnd could California build a framework that protects agriculture—\nallows innovation—\nrequires transparency—\nand gives people the right to choose?\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat conversation naturally leads somewhere deeper.\nBecause In-N-Out’s story has never been only about hamburgers.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThe company’s public identity has long emphasized quality, customers, Associates, family, service, and community.\nAnd the Snyder family has invested in causes involving children, abuse and neglect, human trafficking, substance abuse, and recovery.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat makes Lynsi relevant to a much larger conversation about family, freedom, responsibility, and community.\n\n[GEORGE]\nBecause Viral Fusion is ultimately about agency.\n\n[GEORGE]\nWe are not asking politicians to decide what families should believe.\nWe are building a mechanism that can bring farmers—\nranchers—\nscientists—\nenvironmental experts—\nbusinesses—\nconsumers—\nand legislative experts into the same process.\nShow the evidence.\nShow the tradeoffs.\nBuild something lawful.\nMake it understandable.\nAnd—\nlet Californians decide.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nCalifornia is where Viral Fusion intends to prove the model.\nThat creates a potentially powerful role for In-N-Out—not simply as a restaurant sponsor, but as a company deeply identified with the state, families, employees, and community.\n\n[GEORGE] (Personal, direct close)\nAnd this is where I would intentionally not tell Lynsi what her role should be.\nBecause she may see something bigger than we do.\nWe can show her the technology—\nthe legislative engine—\nthe statewide launch—\nthe live venues—\nthousands of podcast hosts participating virtually—\nour target relationship with X and xAI—\nand the companies we are bringing into the architecture.\nThen I want to ask her:\nIf this aligns with what you believe—how would you want to be involved?\nWhat issue would you want us to take on?\nWho would you want in the room?\nWhat could In-N-Out contribute?\nAnd what would you want California to become for the next generation of families, employees, farmers, and businesses?\nIf what we are building proves worthy of your trust—\nI do not want to tell you what your role should be.\nI want to ask:\nHow would you want to help shape what comes next?",
