    "narration": "[NARRATOR] (Consequential, grounded)\nEnergy is the infrastructure beneath the infrastructure.\nTransportation depends on it.\nManufacturing depends on it.\nData centers depend on it.\nAI depends on it.\nAnd few people sit closer to the real-world consequences of energy policy than Andy Walz.\nAndy is President of Chevron’s Downstream, Midstream and Chemicals business—\nresponsible for global manufacturing—\nmarketing—\nlubricants—\nchemicals—\nshipping—\npipelines—\nand trading.\nHe has spent decades inside the machinery that turns energy policy into operating reality.\nSo when California changes the rules governing energy infrastructure—\nAndy sees what those decisions actually do.\n\n[GEORGE]\nAnd that is why the conversation I want with Andy is not:\n\"How do we protect Chevron?\"\nAnd it is not:\n\"How do we write policy for one company?\"\nIt is:\nWhat does California actually need from an energy system that must remain affordable, reliable, investable, and capable of evolving?\nWhat regulations are essential?\nWhat timelines are realistic?\nWhere does policy begin discouraging investment?\nWhere do consumers ultimately absorb the consequences?\nAnd where can industry do better?\nPut all of it on the table.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat is exactly the tension Viral Fusion is designed to expose.\nNot through another closed-door negotiation.\nNot through another lobbying campaign.\nAnd not by asking the public to choose between slogans.\nBring in the people who actually understand the problem.\nEngineers.\nEconomists.\nConsumer advocates.\nEnvironmental experts.\nLabor.\nIndustry.\nCommunities.\nAnd the people who know how to turn complex ideas into lawful legislative language.\nThen make the process visible.\nThe economics.\nThe environmental tradeoffs.\nThe consumer consequences.\nThe arguments for—\nand against.\n\n[GEORGE]\nAnd that is the opportunity I want to put in front of Andy and Chevron.\nDon’t just fight the next regulation.\nHelp us build a better arena.\nBring the data.\nBring the engineers.\nBring the operating reality.\nTell us where we're wrong.\nLet environmental groups challenge it.\nLet consumer advocates challenge it.\nLet academics challenge it.\nLet labor challenge it.\nLet legislative experts challenge it.\nAnd then—\nlet’s see what survives.\nIf what survives can become legislation—\ndraft it.\nIf it belongs before the voters—\nbuild the lawful pathway.\nTest it.\nExplain it.\nAnd where the law allows—\nlet Californians decide.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat changes Chevron’s role.\nNot from regulated company to policymaker.\nBut from a company reacting to government—\nto one participating openly in a process designed to produce better-informed public decisions.\nThat could create value for Chevron:\ngreater policy visibility—\nmore durable planning assumptions—\na credible forum for technical evidence—\nand a clearer relationship with the public whose decisions ultimately shape California’s energy future.\n\n[GEORGE] (Direct, personal)\nSo my ask is not simply to Chevron.\nIt is to Andy.\nWill you participate?\nWill you bring Chevron’s engineers—\neconomists—\noperators—\ndata—\nand decades of real-world experience into the room?\nWill you let others challenge it?\nWill you help us identify what California’s energy framework actually needs to get right?\nAnd if something stronger emerges—\nsomething that protects consumers—\nkeeps infrastructure investable—\nsupports reliability—\nand survives public scrutiny—\nwill you help us put that choice in front of Californians?\nBecause the goal is not to protect one company.\nIt is not to protect one technology.\nIt is to build rules strong enough to survive the transition—\nand understandable enough for the public to judge for themselves.\nBuild the framework.\nShow the tradeoffs.\nLet Californians decide.",
