    "narration": "[NARRATOR] (Analytical, consequential)\nPatrick helps us ask how breakthrough medicine reaches the patient.\nBrandon Cuevas helps us answer the next question:\nCan the healthcare system actually afford to carry it there?\nAnd that means confronting questions most healthcare debates never fully resolve.\nWhat actually drives cost?\nWhat did the Affordable Care Act fix—\nand what did it leave fundamentally unresolved?\nWhat is the real healthcare impact of immigration—\nand where does the public debate get the economics wrong?\nThat is why Brandon matters.\n\n[GEORGE]\nI don’t want to ask Brandon:\n\"Why is healthcare expensive?\"\nI want to ask:\nWhere is the money actually going?\nHospital prices?\nAdministration?\nDrug costs?\nProvider consolidation?\nChronic disease?\nRegulation?\nWaste?\nFraud?\nTechnology?\nAnd what lowers total cost—\nversus simply moving that cost somewhere else?\nBecause if we’re going to reform healthcare—\nwe should be able to prove the reform works.\n\n[GEORGE] (More direct)\nAnd that scrutiny has to include Brandon’s own industry.\nWhere is UnitedHealthcare part of the problem?\nWhere does insurance create unnecessary friction?\nWhere do prior authorizations protect patients—\nand where do they simply delay care?\nWhere does consolidation improve efficiency—\nand where does it reduce competition?\nWhere does the payer add value—\nand where does the system need to change?\nIf we are asking everyone else to put their assumptions on the table—UnitedHealthcare has to do the same.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThen Viral Fusion takes the conversation somewhere different.\nNot toward another argument over ideology.\nToward a measurable framework.\nWhat worked under the Affordable Care Act?\nWhat didn’t?\nWhat should remain?\nWhat should change?\nWhat costs are really associated with immigration?\nWho pays them?\nAnd where are those costs overstated—\nor underestimated?\nPut the evidence in front of people.\n\n[GEORGE]\nThen comes the question that matters most:\nCan California create a healthcare framework where doing the right thing for the patient is also the economically rational thing for the system?\nIf better outcomes can also mean:\nlower administrative cost—\nfaster access—\nstronger employer value—\nless waste—\nand more sustainable economics—\nthen that is not a conflict.\nThat is alignment.\n\n[GEORGE]\nAnd let’s take that one step further.\nCan insurer economics and patient health actually point in the same direction?\nBetter diet.\nEarlier detection.\nLess chronic disease.\nFewer preventable hospitalizations.\nBetter management of obesity and diabetes.\nAnd breakthrough treatments—\nlike the cancer therapies Patrick is trying to advance.\nDoes UnitedHealthcare become a stronger business when its members become healthier?\nIf the answer is yes—\nthen maybe one of the most powerful healthcare reforms is not simply changing how we pay for sickness.\nIt is aligning the system around preventing it.\nAnd if that alignment exists—\nhow do we write policy that rewards it?\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat changes the legislative question.\nInstead of asking:\n\"Who should government punish?\"\nask:\nWhat incentives would make better patient outcomes economically rational for everyone involved?\nPatients.\nEmployers.\nProviders.\nTaxpayers.\nInsurers.\nThe answer should not be predetermined.\nThe process should discover it.\n\n[GEORGE]\nThen let’s build it.\nBring in:\npatients—\nproviders—\nemployers—\npayers—\nhospitals—\neconomists—\nregulators—\nnutrition and public-health experts—\nand legislative counsel.\nChallenge the assumptions.\nModel the economics.\nAsk who benefits.\nAsk who bears the cost.\nAsk what happens five years later.\nAnd then ask one central question:\nCan California build a healthcare framework where better health creates better economics?\nIf something survives that scrutiny—\ndraft it.\nTest it.\nExplain it.\nAnd move it through the appropriate lawful process.\n\n[NARRATOR]\nThat makes Brandon more than an industry representative.\nHe becomes one of the people helping determine whether healthcare reform can survive contact with the economics.\nAnd there is value for UnitedHealthcare in participating too.\nLower administrative burden.\nBetter member experience.\nStronger employer relationships.\nMore measurable outcomes.\nA more sustainable cost structure.\nAnd an opportunity to demonstrate that an insurer can create value for itself—\nby creating more value for the people it covers.\n\n[GEORGE] (Direct)\nAnd that is where the ask becomes personal.\nBrandon—\ndo you want to help us build this?\nAnd if the framework is serious—\ndoes UnitedHealthcare want to participate too?\nNot to write its own rules.\nNot to buy an outcome.\nBut to bring:\nits data—\nits actuaries—\nits employer relationships—\nits provider experience—\nits population-health expertise—\nand its understanding of how healthcare is actually financed\ninto the room.\nHelp us identify what works.\nHelp us identify what fails.\nHelp us understand where UnitedHealthcare itself needs to change.\nHelp us avoid unintended consequences.\nAnd if we can build something that:\nimproves access—\nreduces waste—\nprevents disease—\nsupports medical breakthroughs—\nprotects patients—\nand creates better economics for the entire system—\nhelp us turn it into law.\n\n[GEORGE] (Final close)\nBecause Patrick helps us ask how innovation reaches the patient.\nBrandon helps us determine whether the economics can carry it there.\nAnd perhaps the larger opportunity is this:\nnot simply building a system that gets better at paying for disease—\nbut building one that has a financial reason to create less disease in the first place.\nIf we can align:\npatient—\nprovider—\nemployer—\ntaxpayer—\npayer—\nand citizen—\naround outcomes that actually improve people’s lives—\nthat is healthcare reform worth building.",
